Now assume that the rates for each of 7 valid transitions in the figure 
is constant over some
period $(s,t)$, with event rates $q_{jk} = exp(\eta_{jk})$ for the $j:k$
transition, 0 for transitions that cannot occur directly such as 0MC:2MC.
Define the nstate by nstate hazard matrix $H$ as
\begin{align}
H_{jk} &= \left{ \array{cc} exp(\eta_{jk}) & Q_{jk} =1 \\
             0 & Q_{jk}=0, j\ne k \end{array} \right. \label{qfill}\\
\sum_kH_{jk} &=0 \label{hdiag} \\
 P(s,t) &= e^H
\end{align}
Equation \ref{qfill} state that row sums of $H$ are zero, 
which defines the diagonal elements of $H$; the diagonal of $Q$ is always 0.
$P(s,t)$ is the probability transition matrix from $s$ to $t$, where exp is
the matrix exponential.

The exponential of a matrix is defined parallel to the simple exponential
\begin{align*}
\exp(A) &= I + A + A^2/2! + A^3/3! + \ldots \\
        &= lim_{n \rightarrow \infty} (I + A/n)^n
\end{align*}
The function has a reputation for computational difficulty \cite{Dongarra},
but modern software is quite good, and it turns out that transition matrices
are one of the more benign cases for the algorithms.
Nevetheless, repeated compuation of matrix exponentials consumes most of the
computational time for an icmsh fit.

If the process is Markov, i.e., transition rates at any time $t$ depend only
on the true state at time $t$ and not otherwise on the past, then
\begin{equation}
P(t_0, t_1) P(t_1, t_2) \ldots P(t_{k-1}, t_k) = P(t_0, t_k)
\end{equation}
If there were 4 states and 7 time points, there are $4^7 > 16000$ different
possible sample paths, but they are all summarized by this simple matrix
multiplication.

The likelihood can be computed separately for each subject $i$, and over the 
next 2 paragraphs we suppress any $i$ subscript for simplicity.
Let $\alpha(t)$ be a vector with one element per state, containing the estimated
probability in state for the subject at time $t$.
Using timeline data, the initial value for $\alpha$ will be found in the first
data row for a subject, i.e., their initial state and
$\alpha(t)= (0, 0, ldots, 1, 0, \ldots 0)$, i.e, a 1 in the observed intial 
state.
Now march forward over time intervals $(t_0, t_1, ....t_k)$ for this subject
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Compute $H(t_0)$ using covariate value at time $t_0$
  \item Set $\alpha(t_1) = \alpha(t_0) \exp[(t_1-t_0) H(t_0)]$
    \begin{itemize}
      \item If censored at $t_1$ then $\alpha(t_1)= \alpha(t_0)$
      \item If observed to be in state $k$ at $t_1$, set $\alpha_j=0$ for all
        $j\ne k$. (The probability of being in a different state is 0.)
      \item If dead at $t_1$ then  $\alpha(t_1)= \alpha(t_0) D$, where 
        $D$ is a copy of $H(t_0)$ with all columns zeroed except the 
        death state.
    \end{itemize}
  \item Compute $H(t_1)$, $P(t_1,t_2)$ and $\alpha(t_2)$  
    using covariate values at $t_1$
  \item Update $\alpha(t_2)$ based on the state at $t_2$
   \item ...
\end{enumerate}

This can be written as
$$
 \alpha_0 P(t_0,t_1) D_1 P(t_1, t_2) D_2 \ldots D_k
$$
where each $D$ matrix is either the identity, if that row is censored, 
$D$ is diagonal with $d_{kk}=1$ if this is an interval censored observation
observed to be in state $k$,
and if death is observed the non-diagonal matrix
\begin{equation}
 D_{jk} = \left{ \begin{array}{cc}
        H_{jk} & j /ne k, k= \rm{death} \\
        0 , otherwise
        \end{array} \right.
\end{equation}
The final result will be a vector with nstate elements, the likelihood for this
subject is the sum of the elements.

Two further notes are that it is possible to have exact states other than
death, e.g., a surgical procedure's timing will be known.  Death is different,
however, in that at any censoring time we can set $\alpha_{death}=0$: any 
observation of the subject implies that they are not dead.
